   "subject": "2-block PLS with single variable",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">Hi all,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">I have a somewhat silly question. When running a 2-block PLS between a landmark dataset (shape3D) and a single variable (BF), does the order of the blocks matter? </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">I ask because the function runs smoothly with <i>phylo.integration(<b>BF, shape3D</b>, tree_prune, iter=999)</i></div><div class=\"gmail_default\"><div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div><div><font face=\"georgia, serif\">but running<i> phylo.integration(</i></font><i style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><b>shape3D</b></i><font face=\"georgia, serif\"><i><b>, BF</b> , tree_prune, iter=999) </i>leds to this error: Error in y %*% V : non-conformable arguments</font></div><div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">I am trying to examine how cranial shape is associated with bite force (BF), essentially hoping to find a linear combination of shape variables that predicts BF. So I&#39;m hoping to run a model that resembles BF ~ shape3D.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">I greatly appreciate your help.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">Best,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\">Chris</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:georgia,serif\"><br></div><div><div class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-size:small\"><div><b><br></b></div><div><b>Chris Law<div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif;display:inline\">​ </div></b><span style=\"font-size:12.6667px\">|</span><b><div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif;display:inline\"> ​</div></b><span style=\"font-size:12.6667px\">Graduate Student</span><b><div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif;display:inline\"> </div></b><span style=\"font-size:12.6667px\">|</span><b><div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif;display:inline\"> ​</div></b><span style=\"font-size:12.6667px\">University of California, Santa Cruz</span><div style=\"font-size:12.6667px;font-family:georgia,serif;display:inline\">​ </div></div><div><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\">Coastal Biology Building<div style=\"font-family:georgia,serif;display:inline\">​</div></span></div></div><div style=\"font-size:small\">130 McAllister Way </div><div style=\"font-size:small\">Santa Cruz, CA 95060</div><a href style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204);font-size:small\" data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">cj...@ucsc.edu</a><span style=\"font-size:small\"> </span><span style=\"font-size:12.6667px\">|</span><span style=\"font-size:12.6667px\"> </span><a href=\"http://research.pbsci.ucsc.edu/eeb/cjlaw/\" style=\"color:rgb(17,85,204);font-size:12.8px\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://research.pbsci.ucsc.edu/eeb/cjlaw/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792485866422000&amp;usg=AOvVaw35OKSO_QJfJXmkBnSvJlj3\">http://research.pbsci.ucsc.edu/eeb/cjlaw/</a><span style=\"font-size:12.8px\"> <br></span><br></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>\r\n</div>\r\n"
    ]
