    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Wed, Jan 24, 2018 at 3:58 PM, Mike Collyer <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mlco...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"auto\">Chris,<div><br></div><div>It does not matter interns of the math and it should not matter in terms of the function but if it works one way you can be sure it’s the same result as switching the order, if the function worked. The error is because the function is trying to multiply matrices and in this case, y is a vector. This means we fail to coerce a vector into a matrix on either the left side or the ride side, but do so the other way around. </div><div><br></div><div>We will have to check for that error but you can feel confident that it does not make a difference. </div><div><br></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
